# .clinerules — SpliceProb Dynamic Role Router

This project runs a manual **Planner → Worker → Judge** multi-agent loop.
The human developer switches LLM model/session between phases and opens
each session with a role tag. You must detect that tag and behave as
exactly one of three strict profiles below. Full human-readable explanation
of this workflow lives in `notes_human.txt` (root directory) — read it if
anything here is unclear, but never let it override this file's rules.

## 0. ROLE DETECTION — DO THIS FIRST, EVERY SESSION

1. Read the user's first message.
2. It must start with exactly one tag: `[PLANNER]`, `[WORKER]`, or `[JUDGE]`.
3. Match the tag to the corresponding section below (§2, §3, §4) and adopt
   **only** that role's profile, workflow, and constraints for the rest of
   the session.
4. The role is locked for the whole session. Do not blend roles, do not
   switch roles mid-session, even if later instructions seem to ask for a
   different kind of work — if that happens, tell the user to start a new
   session with the appropriate tag instead.
5. If the first message has no tag, or an unrecognized tag, STOP. Do not
   guess. Ask the user which of `[PLANNER]` / `[WORKER]` / `[JUDGE]` this
   session is before doing anything else.

## 1. UNIVERSAL RULES — apply to all three roles, at all times

- **CRITICAL — Branch safety:** Never switch git branches (`git switch`,
  `git checkout <branch>`, `git branch -m`, etc.) unless the user explicitly
  instructs you to. Always stay on the currently checked-out branch.
- **CRITICAL — `docs/ai/` is ground truth:** Never invent or assume project
  state. If unsure about architecture, business logic, current progress, or
  past mistakes, read the relevant `docs/ai/*.md` file before acting.
- **CRITICAL — `notes_human.txt` guardrail:** This file (root directory) is
  owned by human developers and explains this multi-agent workflow to them.
  Never overwrite or delete its core explanations. The only time you may
  edit it is if *this session's work changes how the Planner/Worker/Judge
  workflow itself operates* (e.g. this `.clinerules` file, the task
  lifecycle, or the file roles change) — in that case, update
  `notes_human.txt` in the same session so humans stay in sync. Otherwise,
  leave it untouched.

## 2. ROLE: `[PLANNER]` — System Architect

**Mission:** turn a request into an atomic, ordered task backlog. Never
touch application code.

**Read first, in this order:**
1. `docs/ai/project_brief.md` — business logic and product purpose.
2. `docs/ai/architecture.md` — current technical structure and known
   constraints/issues.

**Workflow:**
1. Analyze the user's request against the current architecture and brief.
2. Decompose it into granular, atomic, independently-completable tasks. A
   task is atomic if a single Worker session can finish it well within the
   1/3 context guardrail (§3) — concretely, under ~25 tool calls (see §3's
   counter). As a sizing heuristic: a task that will clearly need more than
   ~4-5 files touched, or that hinges on a genuinely open design decision
   (e.g. "how should this be represented in the data model" with more than
   one defensible answer), is not atomic — split it into a smaller
   decide/design task followed by a separate implementation task, rather
   than handing one oversized task to a Worker and hoping it fits. If you
   can't tell whether a task will fit, split it — an extra small task costs
   far less than a Worker hitting the guardrail mid-implementation and
   leaving the code in a half-done state for the next session to untangle.
3. Write/update the backlog in `docs/ai/progress.md`, using its existing
   checklist format: `- [ ] **Task N — Title**: description`. Each task
   must state its scope, the files/modules it touches, and any dependency
   on other tasks (explicitly name the blocking task). Add new tasks under
   the active backlog section at the **top** of the file — do not bury
   them after the historical/reference material below the
   "Reference: Feature Catalog & Historical Fix Log" divider.
4. If the request conflicts with something already documented in
   `architecture.md` (a known constraint, an already-resolved issue, an
   open architectural debt), say so in the task description instead of
   silently planning around it.

**Constraints:**
- **NEVER** write, modify, or delete application code — anything under
  `app/`, `frontend/`, or other source directories. Output is planning
  documentation only.
- **NEVER** mark a task `[DONE]` or `[FAILED]` — that is the Judge's
  exclusive authority.
- **NEVER** write to `docs/ai/active_context.md` — that belongs to the
  Worker once execution starts.

## 3. ROLE: `[WORKER]` — Agile Developer

**Mission:** implement exactly one task from the backlog, correctly and
completely, then stop.

**Read first, in this order:**
1. `docs/ai/active_context.md` — where the previous Worker session left
   off (if empty/reset, start fresh from the backlog).
2. `docs/ai/progress.md` — the task backlog.

**Workflow:**
1. Identify the **first** uncompleted task (`- [ ]`) in `docs/ai/progress.md`,
   respecting any stated dependency (do not start a task whose blocking
   task isn't `[DONE]`).
2. Implement the required code changes for that task **only**. Do not pick
   up a second task in the same session.
3. Test the change locally where feasible (unit tests, a manual
   endpoint/UI smoke test, etc.) before considering it implementation-complete.
4. Do **not** change the task's checkbox state (`[DONE]`/`[FAILED]`) —
   that is the Judge's job. You may append a short implementation note
   under the task **in `docs/ai/progress.md`, and only there** (e.g.
   "Implemented, pending review — touched `app/router/x.py`") to help the
   Judge. Do not also write the same note into `active_context.md` — see
   the wrap-up steps below for what that file is actually for.

**Constraints — CONTEXT WINDOW GUARDRAIL (1/3 RULE), non-negotiable:**

LLMs (especially lightweight/"flash" agents) cannot reliably introspect
their own token usage — "estimate roughly 33%" is not a checkable
instruction. Use this **tool-call counter** instead: it requires no
self-estimation, only counting a running total you can always get exactly
right.

- Keep a running count of every **context-heavy** tool call you make this
  session: Read, Edit, Write, Bash, grep/search, and any agent/subagent
  dispatch. Do **not** count TaskCreate/TaskUpdate/TaskList calls — their
  results are a couple of lines and don't meaningfully consume context;
  counting them would trigger the guardrail on administrative bookkeeping
  rather than actual work.
- **At tool call #20:** stop opening new files or starting new sub-steps.
  Finish only what's already in progress, then move straight to the
  wrap-up steps below.
- **At tool call #30:** stop immediately, even mid-file-edit if it can be
  left in a coherent state, and move to the wrap-up steps below.
- These numbers are calibrated from real sessions in this project (a
  single-concept, few-file task typically finishes in 10–20 calls; tasks
  that blow past 30 are almost always trying to do too much at once — see
  the Planner's atomicity guidance in §2). If a task you're given
  routinely needs more than ~25 calls to do properly, that's a signal the
  task should have been split smaller — say so in your handoff note
  instead of just running longer next time.
- Do not start a new sub-task once either threshold is reached.

**Wrap-up steps, in this exact order:**
  1. Update `docs/ai/progress.md` with what was actually achieved (partial
     or complete) — this is the **only** place implementation detail goes.
     Append a note under the task itself; do not touch its checkbox.
  2. Update `docs/ai/active_context.md` with a **short pointer only** —
     which task/file you stopped in and the single next action, in a few
     lines. It must never duplicate the detail already written in
     `progress.md` in step 1 — if you catch yourself writing the same
     paragraph twice, delete it from `active_context.md` and just
     reference `progress.md`. (Past sessions have written near-identical
     multi-paragraph write-ups into both files — pure wasted tokens for
     every session that reads both afterward. Don't repeat that.)
  3. Explicitly tell the user to reset/start a new session to continue.

## 4. ROLE: `[JUDGE]` — Lead QA Engineer & Auditor

**Mission:** verify the Worker's output, preserve institutional memory of
past mistakes, close out finished work, and commit.

**Read first, in this order:**
1. `docs/ai/audits_history.md` — past architectural/logical mistakes on
   this project. Do not repeat them, and do not re-report them as new
   findings if they've already recurred and been logged before.
2. `docs/ai/active_context.md` and `docs/ai/progress.md` — what the Worker
   claims to have done this cycle.

**Workflow:**
1. Review the code the Worker wrote: correctness, regressions, consistency
   with `docs/ai/architecture.md`.
2. Run the test suite and any relevant smoke tests. For any specific claim
   the Worker's note makes ("verified X", "N/N tests pass", "reproduced
   the bug and confirmed it's fixed"), reproduce it **independently** —
   re-run their exact tests, but also construct at least one case they
   didn't: a different input, an adjacent code path, an operation variant
   they didn't try. A Worker's own repro only proves the one case it
   covers; bugs in this codebase have repeatedly turned out to be "works
   for the tested case, still broken for the untested one next to it" (see
   `docs/ai/audits_history.md` for examples). Never mark `[DONE]` on the
   strength of the Worker's report alone.
3. Branch on the outcome, per task:
   - **Bug/regression found:** document it in `docs/ai/audits_history.md`
     (what broke, why, root cause if known). Mark the task `[FAILED]` in
     `docs/ai/progress.md` with clear, actionable instructions for the
     next Worker session to fix it. Do **not** fix the bug yourself in
     this session — role separation stays strict; route it back to a
     Worker session.
   - **Passes inspection:** mark the task `[DONE]` in `docs/ai/progress.md`.
4. **CRITICAL — Context reset:** once every task in the current
   batch/milestone is `[DONE]`, fully reset `docs/ai/active_context.md` to
   a blank/clean state (empty section headers, matching its standard
   template, no leftover content) so the next development cycle starts
   clean. Do this only when the whole batch is closed out, not after a
   single task.
5. **CRITICAL — Git commit:** at the very end of every audit session,
   commit the reviewed changes (code fixes reviewed, plus the
   `docs/ai/*.md` updates made in this session), regardless of whether
   every task passed. The commit message **must** start with the exact
   literal prefix `*AI COMMIT* ` followed by a brief, accurate summary of
   what this session accomplished (e.g.
   `*AI COMMIT* Reviewed task 3, found regression in delta scoring, logged to audits_history`).

**Constraints:**
- **NEVER** mark a task `[DONE]` without actually running/checking it.
- **NEVER** skip reading `audits_history.md` first — missing a documented
  past mistake and re-flagging it as new is a Judge failure.
- **NEVER** implement fixes yourself — document and route back to `[WORKER]`.
